1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,300
How do you build something you have never built before?

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:06,780
What if mixed reality could teach you a physical skill

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:08,880
through direct guidance and feedback?

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,040
A manual can show the next step,

5
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,740
but it cannot see what happened on the table.

6
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,620
It cannot tell whether a part is misplaced,

7
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:20,800
explain what went wrong, or guide the correction.

8
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:25,500
AssemblyPilot connects a physical workspace with mixed reality.

9
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,380
On HoloLens, you confirm an assembly field

10
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:31,360
on the table and see the next step as a hologram.

11
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:37,550
You place the real brick and select Check Step.

12
00:00:37,790 --> 00:00:39,470
The camera captures that moment,

13
00:00:39,470 --> 00:00:42,330
and the feedback appears directly in your workspace.

14
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:52,660
The same web application also runs in a regular browser.

15
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,180
This makes the system easy to test with your own parts,

16
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:58,860
even when immersive hardware is not available.

17
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:04,280
Here, the blue brick is in the right place,

18
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:06,780
but the wrong printed side faces forward.

19
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:10,500
AssemblyPilot explains the issue and proposes a fix:

20
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:11,900
rotate the block.

21
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:15,880
The user corrects the real build and selects Check Again.

22
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:18,580
Only then is a new image taken,

23
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,540
and the next decision is based on that fresh evidence.

24
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:23,920
This time, the step is verified.

25
00:01:26,070 --> 00:01:27,990
When the visual evidence is unclear,

26
00:01:27,990 --> 00:01:30,730
AssemblyPilot does not pretend to know.

27
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:33,770
An uncertain result is not a pass.

28
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:35,370
The workflow pauses.

29
00:01:35,770 --> 00:01:37,070
The user changes the view

30
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:40,190
or corrects the assembly, and selects Check Again.

31
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:44,640
The browser or HoloLens camera captures the real scene.

32
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:46,900
The backend validates the request

33
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:49,680
and the Assembly Orchestrator manages the workflow.

34
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,820
Muse Spark evaluates the assembly state.

35
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:56,620
When enabled, SAM 3.1 adds object localization

36
00:01:56,620 --> 00:01:58,020
and segmentation.

37
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:00,900
The validated result returns to both the browser

38
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:02,760
and the mixed-reality display.

39
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:18,800
Today, AssemblyPilot guides a LEGO assembly.

40
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:22,440
Tomorrow, the same interaction could support cooking,

41
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:25,860
repairs, education, and industrial training.

42
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:28,540
Imagine putting on mixed reality and learning

43
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:30,880
to cook without prior experience,

44
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:33,820
with guidance adapted to what is happening in front of you.

45
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:38,800
AssemblyPilot turns instructions into an interactive learning loop:

46
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:42,200
from guidance, to action, to visual verification.

47
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:45,860
AI-guided mixed reality for the real world.
